As the website is currently built, adding a new SOP document requires updating **four parts** of the repository:

1. The editable ODT document
2. The browser-readable PDF
3. The document listing in `search-index.js`
4. The navigation link in `index.html`

The search will not automatically discover a newly uploaded PDF or ODT. The document must be added to the search index.

## Recommended document workflow

Create the new chapter in LibreOffice or another ODT-compatible editor.

For example:

```text
Meeting Procedures.odt
```

Export the same document as a PDF:

```text
meeting-procedures.pdf
```

Use simple PDF filenames containing lowercase letters, numbers, and hyphens. This avoids GitHub Pages path problems.

## 1. Upload the ODT file

Place the editable file inside:

```text
source/
```

Example:

```text
source/Meeting Procedures.odt
```

The ODT filename can contain spaces, although filenames without spaces are easier to maintain.

A cleaner naming approach would be:

```text
source/meeting-procedures.odt
```

## 2. Upload the PDF

Place the exported PDF inside:

```text
documents/
```

Example:

```text
documents/meeting-procedures.pdf
```

The PDF is what the website previews and opens in the browser.

## 3. Add the document to `search-index.js`

Every document has one record inside `search-index.js`. A new document needs an entry similar to this:

```javascript
{
  "id": "meeting-procedures",
  "title": "Meeting Procedures",
  "category": "Board & Governance",
  "description": "Procedures for regular, special, and emergency meetings.",
  "pdf": "./documents/meeting-procedures.pdf",
  "odt": "./source/meeting-procedures.odt",
  "content": "The complete text extracted from the Meeting Procedures document goes here..."
}
```

Each field serves a specific purpose:

| Field         | Purpose                                    |
| ------------- | ------------------------------------------ |
| `id`          | Unique internal name used by navigation    |
| `title`       | Document name shown on the website         |
| `category`    | Section where the document belongs         |
| `description` | Brief library description                  |
| `pdf`         | Path to the browser-readable PDF           |
| `odt`         | Path to the editable source document       |
| `content`     | Full document text searched by the website |

The most important field for full-text search is:

```javascript
"content"
```

The complete text from the ODT must be extracted and inserted there. Merely uploading the ODT does not make its wording searchable.

## 4. Add the document to the sidebar navigation

Open `index.html` and locate the appropriate navigation section.

For example, under:

```html
<span>Board &amp; Governance</span>
```

Add:

```html
<button
  class="nav-link"
  type="button"
  data-view="meeting-procedures"
  data-search="meeting procedures regular special emergency board notice quorum">
  Meeting Procedures
</button>
```

The value of:

```html
data-view="meeting-procedures"
```

must exactly match the document’s `id` in `search-index.js`:

```javascript
"id": "meeting-procedures"
```

The `data-search` words help identify the document in navigation, but the full-text search primarily uses the indexed `content`.

## What happens automatically

Once the document is correctly added to `search-index.js`, the website automatically:

* Adds it to the Document Library
* Searches its title
* Searches its category
* Searches its description
* Searches its complete body text
* Creates matching excerpts
* Provides its PDF and ODT buttons
* Creates its document detail view

You do **not** need to build a separate HTML page for each new SOP.

## When revising an existing SOP

When the wording changes, replace both files:

```text
documents/existing-document.pdf
source/existing-document.odt
```

Then replace the corresponding `content` text inside `search-index.js`.

Without updating `search-index.js`, the website may display the revised PDF while continuing to search the old document wording.

## Best long-term solution

Manually copying the entire document text into `search-index.js` is possible, but it will become cumbersome as the SOP grows.

The better workflow is to use a small index-generation script that:

1. Reads every ODT file in `source/`
2. Extracts the full text automatically
3. Matches it to its PDF
4. Builds `search-index.js`
5. Reports missing PDFs or duplicate IDs

Then your normal process would become:

```text
1. Add the ODT
2. Add the PDF
3. Add one short metadata record
4. Run the index generator
5. Upload the changed files to GitHub
```

For the package I generated, I used this kind of extraction process to index the seven existing documents. The most reliable next improvement would be to add a reusable `build-search-index.py` file and a simple document metadata file, so you never have to manually paste full SOP text into JavaScript.
